WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.800
(gentle instrumental music)

00:00:01.800 --> 00:00:04.168
- [Warrick] We're four days
walk from the nearest road.

00:00:04.168 --> 00:00:08.503
The landscape's vast, the
wilderness is almost unlimited.

00:00:08.503 --> 00:00:12.670
The access is by aircraft,
on foot, or on the right day,

00:00:14.198 --> 00:00:17.516
you can boat it, but it's
a challenge in itself.

00:00:17.516 --> 00:00:21.118
Really, we are at the mercy
of the weather and the tides

00:00:21.118 --> 00:00:23.218
in our day to day life.

00:00:23.218 --> 00:00:26.365
It's difficult living out here,
but if you do what's needed

00:00:26.365 --> 00:00:27.950
and everything comes together,

00:00:27.950 --> 00:00:30.950
it's a really rewarding place to be.

00:00:37.546 --> 00:00:40.056
- [Narrator] Warrick
Mitchell lived in Fiordland,

00:00:40.056 --> 00:00:43.834
New Zealand's oldest and
largest national park.

00:00:43.834 --> 00:00:46.798
It covers over 12,000 square kilometers,

00:00:46.798 --> 00:00:49.843
but is home to only a few dozen people.

00:00:49.843 --> 00:00:51.896
The park's massive mountain range

00:00:51.896 --> 00:00:55.446
isolates Warrick from civilization.

00:00:55.446 --> 00:00:56.279
- [Warrick] Living out here,

00:00:56.279 --> 00:00:59.466
like everywhere, you need
shelter, a good dry camp.

00:00:59.466 --> 00:01:02.768
(lively instrumental music)

00:01:02.768 --> 00:01:05.202
We have solar panel for power, lighting,

00:01:05.202 --> 00:01:07.854
and our satellite communications.

00:01:07.854 --> 00:01:09.050
We have a quad bike,

00:01:09.050 --> 00:01:11.345
we have two boats for fishing and diving,

00:01:11.345 --> 00:01:14.312
and we have the freezer
which keeps our food

00:01:14.312 --> 00:01:15.938
and our produce cold.

00:01:15.938 --> 00:01:18.801
We rely on rainwater for drinking water,

00:01:18.801 --> 00:01:20.889
so that's not too hard

00:01:20.889 --> 00:01:24.778
with seven meters of rainfall annually.

00:01:24.778 --> 00:01:26.248
We largely live off the land.

00:01:26.248 --> 00:01:27.416
When you're living off the land,

00:01:27.416 --> 00:01:30.875
you're really at the mercy of
the weather and the elements,

00:01:30.875 --> 00:01:34.278
so there's certain times
that will allow you to go out

00:01:34.278 --> 00:01:38.445
and harvest a deer or catch
a fish, capture the menu.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.246
If you have a down day and
the waves are really good,

00:01:49.246 --> 00:01:51.482
you're more than likely gonna go surfing.

00:01:51.482 --> 00:01:52.404
If the waves aren't good,

00:01:52.404 --> 00:01:55.055
but the river's looking nice,
you might catch a trout.

00:01:55.055 --> 00:01:56.831
If the waves are flat
but the ocean's calm,

00:01:56.831 --> 00:01:58.808
you might go sea fishing.

00:01:58.808 --> 00:02:01.688
If the ocean's stormy,
you might go up the river

00:02:01.688 --> 00:02:03.338
for a kayak or go through the bush

00:02:03.338 --> 00:02:05.404
and see if you can spot a deer.

00:02:05.404 --> 00:02:08.503
(gun firing)

00:02:08.503 --> 00:02:10.424
Living in the wilderness
doesn't mean you're living

00:02:10.424 --> 00:02:12.296
in isolation from people.

00:02:12.296 --> 00:02:14.785
We certainly couldn't be doing what we do

00:02:14.785 --> 00:02:17.302
if it wasn't for the
pilots and our neighbors

00:02:17.302 --> 00:02:18.469
and our friends.

00:02:18.469 --> 00:02:20.758
Whether it's the harshness
of the environment

00:02:20.758 --> 00:02:23.220
and being so isolated that
brings people together

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:25.972
or the fact that the
people that are willing

00:02:25.972 --> 00:02:29.002
to travel this far have
like minded passion

00:02:29.002 --> 00:02:31.030
for the environment and the outdoors,

00:02:31.030 --> 00:02:32.524
one thing's for sure over the years

00:02:32.524 --> 00:02:35.942
is that the community's
become really tight knit.

00:02:35.942 --> 00:02:37.762
Can you go ahead and
turn this main switch on

00:02:37.762 --> 00:02:41.095
and we'll just see if she wants to work?

00:02:42.275 --> 00:02:44.147
Oh, no.

00:02:44.147 --> 00:02:47.111
When things break or
things don't go your way,

00:02:47.111 --> 00:02:48.255
it's always really important

00:02:48.255 --> 00:02:51.531
to remember you're in the green.

00:02:51.531 --> 00:02:53.078
The experience is being out here

00:02:53.078 --> 00:02:54.353
and enjoying being out here.

00:02:54.353 --> 00:02:57.303
Everything else is a bonus,
so you can't get too serious

00:02:57.303 --> 00:02:59.799
if one thing breaks.

00:02:59.799 --> 00:03:01.931
I don't know too many places in the world

00:03:01.931 --> 00:03:04.452
where you can stand on a boat looking back

00:03:04.452 --> 00:03:06.907
and you can just see ocean
leading into forest leading

00:03:06.907 --> 00:03:09.899
into massive snow-capped
mountains and glacier-shaped bays

00:03:09.899 --> 00:03:13.046
with pristine rivers and clear water.

00:03:13.046 --> 00:03:16.084
The trees are as they were 200 years ago,

00:03:16.084 --> 00:03:17.438
and the people that I bring out here,

00:03:17.438 --> 00:03:19.674
they get to experience
this nature as it was

00:03:19.674 --> 00:03:22.754
and as it should be, and
we try to keep the area

00:03:22.754 --> 00:03:24.430
and the environment pristine

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:27.398
in this little corner of New Zealand.

00:03:27.398 --> 00:03:31.148
(gentle instrumental music)

